cin >> m>> n;
char a[m][n] = {};

getchar(); --------------------->> 1st getchar();

for(int i=0; i<m ;i++){
    for(int j=0;j<n;j++){
       scanf("%c", &a[i][j]);
   }
getchar(); ----------------------->> 2nd getchar();
}

---------------------------------------------------------------------------

We used -->> scanf("%c") --> Builtin function of C -->> Sencitive for input buffer (problem)



Explanation:

1st:
getchar() after cin >> m >> n;
When we input m and n, we press Enter.
That Enter key generates a newline character '\n' in the input buffer.
scanf("%c", &a[i][j]); would read that '\n' as a character, which is unwanted.
So, we use 1st getchar(); to consume the leftover '\n'

2nd:
getchar() inside the for-loop (after each row)
While reading characters for a row, we usually press Enter after typing the row.
That Enter key again stays in the buffer.
The 2nd getchar(); removes this extra '\n', so the next row reads correctly.

